cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use Wormlight, please cite it as below, giving the version you used. Please also cite the connectome it runs,
  Cook et al. 2019 as released in Emmons 2024, and the works most of its synapse signs come from, Fenyves et al. 2020
  and Wang et al. 2024; all four are listed below. The recordings behind its few measured signs are cited in its
  FIDELITY.md.
type: software
title: Wormlight
abstract: >-
  A living C. elegans in the browser. The adult hermaphrodite's connectome, run through a graded neuron model and a
  small, documented set of mechanisms the wiring can't supply, drives a physically simulated body on an agar plate. It
  runs on the GPU in WebGPU, checked against a CPU reference on which every behavioural trial runs. The behavioural
  checkpoints' thresholds were fixed before their results, from published measurements, and every later change is
  logged. On the chosen fit, which gives the model the synapse signs that recordings measure, a resting offset for one
  class of motor neuron and gap junctions that pass current one way, as recordings show, the worm crawls at the first
  checkpoint's partial grade, at about a third of a real worm's speed. The rhythm that paces it comes from its head,
  one of the mechanisms outside the wiring, not from the network, and the worm hardly reverses.
authors:
